<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baijnath, Uttarakhand</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baijnath is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gomti-river">Gomti River</a> in Bageshwar district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known for a group of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temples. It is usually identified with Kartikeyapura, capital of the Katyuri kings, in the Katyur valley. The temples, of the 12th and 13th centuries, are protected monuments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baijnath-uttarakhand.jpg" alt="Baijnath, Uttarakhand" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shikharsaxena1618 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baijnath_Dham_with_Himalayas_in_the_backdrop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Baijnath lies in the Katyur valley of the Kumaon Himalayas, on the left bank of the Gomati River, about 20 km north-west of Bageshwar, at an average height of 1,130 metres. Its temples stand by the confluence of the Gomati and the Garur Ganga, where, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> mythology, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and Parvati were married. The temple complex is dedicated to Shiva as Vaidyanatha, the Lord of Physicians, and holds images of Shiva, Ganesh, Parvati, Chandika, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuver">Kuber</a>, Surya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first permanent settlement was a town called Karvirpur or Karbirpur, on whose ruins the Katyuri king Asanti Deo set up his capital. Local legend says he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joshimath">Joshimath</a> because of a curse laid by Narsingh, while geologists point to landslides and other natural disasters. Under the name Kartikeyapura the town remained the capital of the Katyuri dynasty, who ruled parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> and Doti in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> from the 7th to the 13th century. The main temple is said to have been built by a Katyuri king around 1150.</p><p>After the death of Birdeo, the last king of the united Katyuri kingdom, the realm broke up in the 13th century into eight states. Baijnath stayed with the Baijnath Katyurs, descendants of the old kings, until King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan-chand">Balo Kalyan Chand</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a> annexed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> in 1565. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gorkhas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> overran Kumaon in 1791, and after their defeat in the Anglo-Nepalese War they ceded it to the British by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Treaty of Sugauli</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Baijnath is a small town of Bageshwar district in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>; it had 148 inhabitants in 1901. Its temples are Monuments of National Importance under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a>. An artificial lake near the temple compound was opened on 14 January 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baijnath, Uttarakhand” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tradition places their first seat at Joshimath; their capital Kartikeyapura is usually identified with <b>Baijnath</b> in the Katyur valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/katyuri-kingdom">Katyuri kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
